  {
    "abstract": "We develop a theory of trust in lending that distinguishes between reputation and trust. Banks emerge as more trusted lenders than nonbanks. We show that trust severs the link between performance and the cost and availability of financing for lenders, but trust can be lost and is difficult to regain. Banks survive an erosion of trust better than nonbanks. Banks’ trust advantage arises from the lower cost of funding due to insured deposits and an endogenous belief revision channel that complements the effect of the funding cost advantage. The results have novel policy relevance for deposit insurance scope.",
